{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% import "_macros.html.j2" as m with context %}
{% block title %}Using the images — Capsid Hub{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<header class="page-head">
  <p class="crumbs"><a href="{{ root }}index.html">Capsid Hub</a> / Guides / Using the images</p>
  <h1>Using the images</h1>
  <p class="lede">From a first <code>docker run</code> to a pipeline on an HPC cluster without internet access. Every
    image page in the <a href="{{ root }}catalogue/index.html">catalogue</a> repeats the commands below with its own
    reference filled in.</p>
</header>

<nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#references">References</a></li>
    <li><a href="#local">Docker and Podman</a></li>
    <li><a href="#apptainer">Apptainer</a></li>
    <li><a href="#nextflow">Nextflow</a></li>
    <li><a href="#snakemake">Snakemake</a></li>
    <li><a href="#hpc">HPC clusters</a></li>
    <li><a href="#cloud">Kubernetes and cloud</a></li>
    <li><a href="#stacks">Stacks</a></li>
    <li><a href="#reporting">Reporting in a paper</a></li>
    <li><a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a></li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<section class="numbered" id="references">
  <h2><span class="secno">1</span>Image references</h2>
  <div class="body">
{{ m.code(REGISTRY ~ "/<tool>:<version>          # one tool version\n" ~ REGISTRY ~ "/<stack>:<version>         # a stack of tools\n" ~ REGISTRY ~ "/<tool>@sha256:<digest>    # immutable", "text") }}
    <p>Each reference is a multi-architecture index: the runtime picks <code>linux/amd64</code> or
      <code>linux/arm64</code> (Apple silicon, AWS Graviton, Ampere) automatically. A tag can be republished after a
      security update of the runtime layer; a digest cannot. Use tags while developing and digests in anything you
      publish. Image pages list both; <a href="{{ root }}catalog.json"><code>catalog.json</code></a> lists every
      reference for scripts.</p>
    <p>There is no <code>latest</code> tag. A pipeline always names the version it was validated with.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="local">
  <h2><span class="secno">2</span>Docker and Podman</h2>
  <div class="body">
{{ m.code("docker run --rm -u \"$(id -u):$(id -g)\" -v \"$PWD:/data\" -w /data \\\n  " ~ REGISTRY ~ "/samtools:1.25 \\\n  samtools flagstat sample.bam", "sh") }}
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><code>-u "$(id -u):$(id -g)"</code> writes output files as you rather than as the image user (1000).</li>
      <li><code>-v "$PWD:/data" -w /data</code> mounts the current directory and runs from it.</li>
      <li>Podman accepts the same flags; in rootless Podman, <code>--userns=keep-id</code> replaces <code>-u</code>.</li>
      <li>For an interactive shell: <code>docker run --rm -it … bash</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each image documents itself. These files are inside every image:</p>
    <table class="booktabs">
      <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">/etc/capsid/recipe.yaml</td><td>the recipe the image was built from</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">/etc/capsid/pixi.lock, requirements.lock</td><td>the locked closure</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">/etc/capsid/sbom.spdx.json</td><td>SPDX 2.3 software bill of materials</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">/etc/capsid/ro-crate-metadata.json</td><td>RO-Crate (JSON-LD) description for FAIR archiving</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">/etc/capsid/module.nf, rule.smk</td><td>a Nextflow module and a Snakemake rule for the image</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
{{ m.code("docker run --rm " ~ REGISTRY ~ "/samtools:1.25 cat /etc/capsid/recipe.yaml", "sh") }}
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="apptainer">
  <h2><span class="secno">3</span>Apptainer and Singularity</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>Run straight from the registry (Apptainer converts and caches the image), or convert once into a SIF file:</p>
{{ m.code("apptainer exec docker://" ~ REGISTRY ~ "/samtools:1.25 samtools --version\n\napptainer pull samtools-1.25.sif docker://" ~ REGISTRY ~ "/samtools:1.25\napptainer exec --cleanenv -B /scratch samtools-1.25.sif samtools --version", "sh") }}
    <p>Images work under <code>--cleanenv</code> and <code>--containall</code> because <code>PATH</code> is set inside
      the image, and under any UID because tool trees are world-readable and caches go to <code>/tmp</code>.
      <code>capsid export def &lt;tool&gt;@&lt;version&gt;</code> writes a definition file with a <code>%test</code>
      section for sites that build SIF images from definitions.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="nextflow">
  <h2><span class="secno">4</span>Nextflow</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>Assigning an image to processes needs one block in <code>nextflow.config</code>:</p>
{{ m.code("process {\n    withName: 'SAMTOOLS_SORT|SAMTOOLS_INDEX' {\n        container = '" ~ REGISTRY ~ "/samtools:1.25'\n    }\n}", "groovy") }}
    <p>The CLI can generate an nf-core-style module (inputs <code>tuple val(meta), path(reads)</code>, <code>task.ext.args</code>,
      <code>versions.yml</code>, <code>stub</code>) and a configuration with one profile per runtime:</p>
{{ m.code("capsid export nextflow samtools@1.25 --out modules/local/samtools.nf\ncapsid export config --target samtools@1.25 --out nextflow.config\n\nnextflow run main.nf -profile docker\nnextflow run main.nf -profile apptainer\nnextflow run main.nf -profile slurm,apptainer", "sh") }}
    <table class="booktabs">
      <thead><tr><th>Profile</th><th>Settings</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">docker</td><td><code>docker.enabled</code>; runs as the calling user</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">podman</td><td><code>podman.enabled</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">apptainer, singularity</td><td>enabled with <code>autoMounts</code>; add binds in <code>runOptions</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">slurm</td><td><code>process.executor = 'slurm'</code>; set queue and account in the commented lines</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The generated module's script is a template (<code>&lt;tool&gt; $args $reads &gt; prefix.out</code>): edit inputs,
      outputs and the command for your step.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="snakemake">
  <h2><span class="secno">5</span>Snakemake</h2>
  <div class="body">
{{ m.code("rule bwa_mem:\n    input: ref=\"ref.fa\", reads=\"reads/{sample}.fq.gz\"\n    output: \"aligned/{sample}.sam\"\n    threads: 8\n    container: \"docker://" ~ REGISTRY ~ "/bwa:0.7.19\"\n    shell: \"bwa mem -t {threads} {input.ref} {input.reads} > {output}\"", "python") }}
{{ m.code("snakemake --sdm apptainer --cores 8\nsnakemake --sdm apptainer --apptainer-args \"-B /scratch\" --cores 8\n\ncapsid export snakemake bwa@0.7.19 --out rules/bwa.smk   # a complete rule to adapt", "sh") }}
    <p>Snakemake runs containers through Apptainer. <code>--sdm</code> is the short form of
      <code>--software-deployment-method</code> (Snakemake ≥ 8).</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="hpc">
  <h2><span class="secno">6</span>HPC clusters</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Bind points exist.</strong> <code>/scratch</code>, <code>/data</code>, <code>/work</code>, <code>/lustre</code>
        and <code>/shared</code> are present in every image, so <code>-B /scratch</code> works even with read-only images.</li>
      <li><strong>Offline compute nodes.</strong> Pull SIF files on a login node, or mirror images to an internal registry
        with all architectures and the original digest, so they can still be verified against this hub's signatures:</li>
    </ul>
{{ m.code("skopeo copy --all docker://" ~ REGISTRY ~ "/samtools:1.25 \\\n  docker://registry.example.org/capsid/samtools:1.25", "sh") }}
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Test on the cluster</strong> with the recipe's own acceptance test:
        <code>capsid test recipes/samtools/1.25/recipe.yaml --engine apptainer --sif samtools-1.25.sif</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Single node.</strong> Images do not carry an MPI stack matched to the host; MPI tools are out of scope.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="cloud">
  <h2><span class="secno">7</span>Kubernetes and cloud batch</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>Images satisfy restrictive pod security settings as they are. The same holds for AWS Batch, Google Batch,
      Azure Batch and Nextflow's cloud executors.</p>
{{ m.code("securityContext:\n  runAsNonRoot: true\n  runAsUser: 1000\n  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true      # mount an emptyDir at /tmp\n  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n  capabilities: {drop: [ALL]}", "yaml") }}
    <p>Clusters can refuse unsigned images at admission with the Sigstore policy-controller or Kyverno, using the
      identity shown in each image page's <em>Verification</em> section.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="stacks">
  <h2><span class="secno">8</span>Stacks</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>A <a href="{{ root }}stacks/index.html">stack</a> lets one process pipe several tools together without staging
      intermediate files between containers:</p>
{{ m.code("process CALL_VARIANTS {\n    container '" ~ REGISTRY ~ "/variant-calling-stack:1.0.0'\n    script:\n    \"\"\"\n    samtools sort -@ ${task.cpus} ${bam} | bcftools mpileup -f ${ref} - | bcftools call -mv -Oz -o calls.vcf.gz\n    \"\"\"\n}", "groovy") }}
    <p>Prefer single-tool images otherwise: they are smaller and cached across pipelines.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="reporting">
  <h2><span class="secno">9</span>Reporting in a paper</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>For a methods section, name the tool, its version and the image digest. The digest identifies the exact
      closure listed on the image page and inside the image, and stays resolvable after the tag moves:</p>
    <blockquote class="quote">Reads were aligned with BWA 0.7.19 and sorted with SAMtools 1.25, run from the Capsid images
      <code>{{ REGISTRY }}/bwa@sha256:…</code> and <code>{{ REGISTRY }}/samtools@sha256:…</code>
      (recipes and lockfiles at {{ HUB_URL | replace("https://", "") }}).</blockquote>
    <p>To archive the environment with the data, deposit the image's <code>/etc/capsid/ro-crate-metadata.json</code>
      and <code>sbom.spdx.json</code>, or the SIF file itself, in the same repository as the results.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="troubleshooting">
  <h2><span class="secno">10</span>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <dl class="qa">
      <dt>Output files belong to user 1000.</dt>
      <dd>Run with <code>-u "$(id -u):$(id -g)"</code> (Docker) or <code>--userns=keep-id</code> (rootless Podman). Apptainer runs as you.</dd>
      <dt><code>Permission denied</code> writing into the image.</dt>
      <dd>Tool directories are read-only by design. Write to a mounted directory or to <code>/tmp</code>.</dd>
      <dt><code>no matching manifest for linux/arm64/v8 in the manifest list entries</code>.</dt>
      <dd>The recipe declares only <code>linux/amd64</code>; the image page lists the architectures. Use an amd64 machine, or propose arm64 support.</dd>
      <dt>Apptainer cannot find files outside the working directory.</dt>
      <dd>Bind them: <code>-B /path</code>, or <code>runOptions = '-B /path'</code> in Nextflow, or <code>--apptainer-args "-B /path"</code> in Snakemake.</dd>
      <dt>A tool or version is missing.</dt>
      <dd><a href="{{ root }}guide/contributing/index.html">Submit it</a>, or open an issue with the
        <a href="{{ HUB_URL }}/issues/new/choose">New recipe</a> form.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
